// English: translation by hemistich (r), commentary (c), meanings of difficult words (w) —
// w is aligned by index with the Russian list in verses-a/b/c.js.
export const VERSES_EN = {
  1: { r: ["Says one who hopes for the pardon of a Lord, the All-Hearing —", "Muhammad ibn al-Jazari, the Shafi‘i:"], c: "The author opens by introducing himself, as is customary in didactic poems. He calls himself “one who hopes for pardon” out of humility before Allah, and “All-Hearing” reminds the reader that Allah hears the supplication of whoever asks. Imam Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Jazari (751–833 AH) was the greatest authority on the Qur’anic readings of his age and the author of “an-Nashr fi al-Qira’at al-‘Ashr”; he was born in Damascus, died in Shiraz, and followed the Shafi‘i school.", w: ["hoping, trusting", "pardon; literally the “erasing” of sins", "All-Hearing — one of Allah’s beautiful names", "follower of Imam al-Shafi‘i’s school"] },
  2: { r: ["Praise be to Allah, and may Allah bless", "His Prophet and His chosen one —"], c: "The poem opens with praise of Allah and blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, following the Qur’an, which begins with “al-hamdu lillah,” and the Sunnah. “Mustafa” means “the chosen”: Allah chose Muhammad ﷺ from all creation.", w: ["praise with love and reverence", "blessed; Allah’s salat is His praise of the Prophet before the angels", "His chosen one"] },
  3: { r: ["Muhammad, his family and companions,", "and whoever teaches the Qur’an, and whoever loves it."], c: "The blessing extends to the Prophet’s family and companions ﷺ, to every muqri’ — a teacher who passes on the recitation through a chain of teachers (isnad) — and to everyone who loves the Qur’an or its teacher. From the very first lines the author links the student to an unbroken chain of transmission.", w: ["his family (and followers)", "his companions", "a teacher who transmits the Qur’an with an isnad", "one who loves it"] },
  4: { r: ["To proceed: this is an introduction", "to what its reciter must know."], c: "“Wa ba‘d” marks the move from the opening to the subject. The author calls the poem a “muqaddimah” — an introduction: the essential minimum a reciter starts with before going deeper into the science of readings. Hence its other name, “al-Muqaddimah al-Jazariyyah.”", w: ["“and then” — the move to the subject", "introduction, what comes first", "its (the Qur’an’s) reciter"] },
  5: { r: ["For it is a binding duty upon them,", "before they begin, to learn first"], c: "The author stresses that before reading and studying the readings, a reciter must master the foundations listed in the next verses. Verses 5–8 form a single sentence.", w: ["obligatory", "binding, decided", "beginning (a task)"] },
  6: { r: ["the points of articulation of the letters and their attributes,", "so that they pronounce in the most eloquent of tongues,"], c: "The first two foundations are the makhraj — where a sound comes from — and the sifah — how it sounds. Without them the letters cannot be pronounced as the Arabs pronounced them when the Qur’an was revealed, in the most eloquent language, the language of the Qur’an.", w: ["points of articulation (plural of makhraj)", "qualities, characteristics of letters", "the purest, most eloquent language"] },
  7: { r: ["perfecting tajwid and the places of stopping,", "and what was written in the mushafs:"], c: "Three more foundations follow: tajwid — the rules of pronunciation in connected reading; knowledge of where to stop (waqf); and knowledge of how the mushaf is written (rasm), which determines where and how one may stop.", w: ["carefully perfecting", "places of stopping", "was written (the mushaf’s orthography)", "mushafs, copies of the Qur’an"] },
  8: { r: ["every word written separately or joined in them,", "and the feminine ta that is not written with ha."], c: "This specifies the topic of rasm: words that the mushaf writes joined (mawsul) or separate (maqtu‘), and the feminine ta (ta marbutah) written in some places as an open ta (ت). How one stops on a word depends on this. The final chapters of the poem (verses 79–100) are devoted to these topics.", w: ["written separately", "written joined", "the feminine ta (ta marbutah)", "here: with “ha” (ة/ه)"] },
  9: { r: ["The points of articulation of the letters are seventeen,", "according to what is chosen by whoever has tested it."], c: "Ibn al-Jazari follows al-Khalil ibn Ahmad and most reciters: there are 17 makharij. Other views count 16 (Sibawayh, without the cavity) or 14 (al-Farra’, who joins lam, nun and ra). “Whoever has tested it” is someone who examined pronunciation in practice. The 17 makharij fall into five areas: the cavity (jawf), the throat, the tongue, the lips and the nasal cavity (khayshum).", w: ["points of articulation", "seventeen", "tested, examined by experience"] },
  10: { r: ["Alif is from the cavity (jawf), and its two sisters — they are", "the letters of madd, which end with the air."], c: "The first makhraj is the jawf, the empty space of the mouth and throat. The three madd letters come from it: alif (always preceded by fathah), waw with sukun after dammah, and ya with sukun after kasrah — all three appear in “نُوحِيهَا”. They have no point of contact: the sound lasts as long as the air flows, so they are also called “airy” letters.", w: ["the cavity of the mouth and throat", "its two “sisters” — the waw and ya of madd", "air", "end"] },
  11: { r: ["Then from the deepest part of the throat: hamzah and ha (ه);", "then from its middle: ‘ayn and ha (ح);"], c: "The throat (halq) has three makharij. Its deepest part, farthest from the lips at the vocal folds, produces hamzah (ء) and ha (ه). The middle of the throat, around the epiglottis, produces ‘ayn (ع) and ha (ح). With غ and خ these are called the throat letters.", w: ["farthest (from the lips)", "throat", "its middle"] },
  12: { r: ["from its nearest part: ghayn and kha. And qaf —", "from the back of the tongue, higher up; then kaf —"], c: "The part of the throat nearest the mouth, by the root of the tongue, produces ghayn (غ) and kha (خ). Then come the makharij of the tongue: qaf (ق) is the back of the tongue against the soft palate above it, deeper and higher than kaf. Kaf (ك) is slightly lower and closer to the mouth (continued in the next verse).", w: ["its nearest part (to the mouth)", "the back of the tongue", "higher (closer to the palate)"] },
  13: { r: ["— lower down. From the middle [of the tongue]: jim, shin and ya;", "dad — from its edge where it meets"], c: "Kaf (ك) comes from the back of the tongue below the makhraj of qaf. The middle of the tongue with the hard palate produces jim (ج), shin (ش) and the non-madd ya (ي). Dad (ض) comes from the edge of the tongue meeting the molars (continued in verse 14). It is the hardest sound of Arabic, which is why Arabic is called “the language of dad.”", w: ["lower", "the middle (of the tongue)", "its edge, side", "adjoined"] },
  14: { r: ["the molars, on the left or the right. And lam — from the nearest part [of the edges]", "to their very end."], c: "Dad is pronounced with the edge of the tongue pressed against the upper molars: on the left (easier and more common), on the right, or both at once. Lam (ل) comes from the front parts of both edges of the tongue up to its tip, together with the gums of the upper front teeth.", w: ["molars", "left side", "right side", "the nearest (front) part [of the edges]", "their end, i.e. the tip of the tongue"] },
  15: { r: ["Nun is from its tip — place it below [lam].", "Ra is close to it, but deeper, toward the back [of the tongue]."], c: "Nun (ن) is the tip of the tongue with the gums of the upper incisors, slightly below the makhraj of lam. Ra (ر) is the tip of the tongue closer to its back, slightly deeper than nun. Al-Farra’ counted ل ن ر as one makhraj — hence the view of 14 makharij.", w: ["its (the tongue’s) tip", "below", "close to it", "the back of the tongue", "deeper"] },
  16: { r: ["Ta (ط), dal and ta come from it [the tip] and from", "the [roots of the] upper incisors. And the whistling letters rest"], c: "ط د ت are pronounced with the tip of the tongue touching the roots (gums) of the two upper front incisors. The whistling letters ص ز س are described in the next verse.", w: ["front incisors (two upper and two lower)", "upper", "whistling letters", "residing, located"] },
  17: { r: ["between it [the tip] and the lower incisors, above them.", "And za (ظ), dhal and tha — at the upper [incisors],"], c: "The whistling letters ص ز س come from the tip of the tongue at the lower incisors, above them, with a small gap through which the whistling air passes. ظ ذ ث come from the tip of the tongue with the edges of the upper incisors (continued in verse 18); they are called the interdental letters.", w: ["above", "lower", "upper"] },
  18: { r: ["from the tips of both. And from the inner side of the lip:", "fa, with the edges of the upper incisors."], c: "ظ ذ ث: the tip of the tongue with the edges of the upper incisors. Fa (ف): the inner side of the lower lip with the edges of the upper incisors; “mushrifah” means “elevated,” i.e. the upper ones.", w: ["their tips: of the tongue and the teeth", "the inner side of the (lower) lip", "elevated, i.e. upper"] },
  19: { r: ["From both lips: waw, ba and mim.", "And ghunnah — its makhraj is the nasal cavity (khayshum)."], c: "The lips: ba (ب) and mim (م) by closing the lips, more firmly for ba; the non-madd waw by rounding the lips without closing them. The seventeenth makhraj is the khayshum: the ghunnah, the nasal resonance of nun and mim, comes from it. It is strongest with shaddah, and also in ikhfa’, idgham with ghunnah and iqlab.", w: ["both lips", "ghunnah, nasal sound", "the nasal cavity (its depth)"] },
  20: { r: ["Its attributes: voicing (jahr), softness (rikhwah), lowering (istifal),", "openness (infitah), restraint (ismat) — and name their opposites."], c: "The author lists five attributes, each with an opposite: jahr ↔ hams, rikhwah ↔ shiddah (with tawassut between them), istifal ↔ isti‘la’, infitah ↔ itbaq, ismat ↔ idhlaq. Every letter has exactly one attribute from each pair. He then lists the letters of the opposite attributes; all the others belong to those named here.", w: ["voicing: air does not pass", "softness: the sound flows", "lowered: the tongue does not rise to the palate", "open", "“restrained”, heavy letters", "the opposite"] },
  21: { r: ["Its whispered letters are in “fahaththahu shakhsun sakat”,", "its strong letters in the words “ajid qatin bakat”."], c: "Hams is the flow of air when a letter is pronounced, because of weak reliance on its makhraj: ف ح ث ه ش خ ص س ك ت (10 letters). Shiddah is the blocking of sound because of strong reliance: ء ج د ق ط ب ك ت (8 letters). ك and ت are both whispered and strong: the sound is blocked, and then a light release of air is heard.", w: ["its whispered letters", "its strong letters", "mnemonic of the 10 letters of hams", "mnemonic of the 8 letters of shiddah"] },
  22: { r: ["Between soft and strong are “lin ‘umar”,", "and the seven elevated letters are held in “khussa daghtin qiz”."], c: "Tawassut (in-between): ل ن ع م ر — the sound is partly blocked and partly flows. Isti‘la’ (elevation): خ ص ض غ ط ق ظ — the back of the tongue rises to the palate, so these letters are always pronounced heavy (tafkhim). The remaining 21 letters are lowered.", w: ["between", "elevation (isti‘la’)", "confined, enclosed", "mnemonic of tawassut: ل ن ع م ر", "mnemonic of isti‘la’: خ ص ض غ ط ق ظ"] },
  23: { r: ["Sad, dad, ta (ط) and za (ظ) are covered (itbaq),", "and “firra min lubb” are the fluent letters."], c: "Itbaq (covering): the tongue lies against the palate so that the sound is enclosed between them — ص ض ط ظ. This is the strongest degree of tafkhim. Idhlaq is ease: the letters come from the tip of the tongue or the lips — ف ر م ن ل ب. The others are “restrained”: Arabic has no four- or five-letter roots made only of them, without a fluent letter.", w: ["covered", "“fluent”, light letters (idhlaq)", "mnemonic of idhlaq: ف ر م ن ل ب"] },
  24: { r: ["Its whistling letters are sad, zay and sin;", "qalqalah is in “qutbu jad”; and softness (lin) —"], c: "Here begin the attributes without opposites. Safir is the whistle accompanying ص ز س. Qalqalah is the “echo” of ق ط ب ج د when they carry sukun: because they combine jahr and shiddah, their sound would otherwise not be heard. Qalqalah is strongest at a stop, especially on a doubled letter.", w: ["whistling", "shaking, “echo”", "mnemonic of qalqalah: ق ط ب ج د", "softness"] },
  25: { r: ["— waw and ya with sukun when the letter before them", "has fathah. And deviation (inhiraf) is confirmed"], c: "Lin is the ease and softness of waw and ya with sukun after fathah: “خَوْفٌ”, “بَيْتٌ”. When stopping on such words, lengthening (madd lin) is allowed. Inhiraf is described in the next verse.", w: ["had sukun", "[the letter] before them has fathah", "deviation", "affirmed as correct"] },
  26: { r: ["in lam and ra; and [ra] is given repetition (takrir);", "spreading (tafashshi) belongs to shin; and dad — elongate it (istitalah)."], c: "Inhiraf is the sound turning away from its makhraj: for ل toward the edges of the tongue, for ر toward its back and slightly toward lam. Takrir is the tendency of ra to trill; it is learned in order to avoid it, especially with shaddah (verse 43). Tafashshi is the spreading of air in the mouth with shin. Istitalah is the extension of dad along the whole edge of the tongue.", w: ["repetition, trilling", "spreading (of air)", "elongate — istitalah"] },
  27: { r: ["Applying tajwid is a binding duty:", "whoever does not recite the Qur’an with tajwid is sinful."], c: "The author affirms that practical tajwid is obligatory. Scholars explain that the sin lies in clear errors (lahn jali), which distort a letter, a vowel or the meaning. Hidden errors (lahn khafi) spoil the perfection of recitation and are blameworthy, especially for someone who knows. Studying the theory is a communal obligation, while correct recitation is required of everyone who reads the Qur’an.", w: ["following, applying", "obligatory", "binding", "sinful, committing sin"] },
  28: { r: ["For with it God revealed [the Qur’an],", "and so it has reached us from Him."], c: "The reason it is obligatory: the Qur’an was revealed with tajwid, the Prophet ﷺ taught it so to his companions, and from them it reached us through mass-transmitted chains (tawatur). Tajwid is part of reciting the Qur’an itself, not a later embellishment.", w: ["God (Allah)", "revealed", "reached"] },
  29: { r: ["It is also the adornment of recitation", "and the beauty of delivery and reading."], c: "Beyond being obligatory, tajwid has an aesthetic side: it makes recitation beautiful. “Ada’” is the delivery received from a teacher; “qira’ah” is reading in general.", w: ["adornment", "recitation (of the Qur’an)", "delivery, transmitted recitation"] },
  30: { r: ["Tajwid is giving the letters their right —", "the attributes that belong to them — and what they are due."], c: "The classic definition of tajwid. A letter’s “right” (haqq) is its permanent attributes (jahr, shiddah, isti‘la’ and others), without which it stops being itself. What it is “due” (mustahaqq) are the temporary qualities that arise from the permanent ones depending on position: tafkhim and tarqiq, idgham, ikhfa’, madd and so on.", w: ["giving", "its right — permanent attributes", "what it is entitled to — temporary qualities"] },
  31: { r: ["and returning each [letter] to its origin (makhraj),", "and pronouncing each like its counterparts."], c: "Tajwid also means pronouncing each letter from its makhraj, and consistency: the same letter in the same conditions always sounds the same — the same length of madd, the same degree of ghunnah and tafkhim throughout the recitation.", w: ["returning", "its origin, i.e. makhraj", "its like"] },
  32: { r: ["fully, without any affectation,", "gently in pronunciation, without forcing."], c: "The perfection of tajwid lies in naturalness: no exaggeration (over-lengthening, extra pressure, “chewing” the letters) and no carelessness. Scholars warn against both falling short and going too far.", w: ["complete, full", "affectation, artificiality", "gentleness", "forcing, excess"] },
  33: { r: ["And between it and abandoning it there is nothing", "but a person’s exercise of his jaw."], c: "The path to tajwid is practice: repetition under a teacher, “training the jaw,” that is, the organs of speech. Knowing the rules without practice does not produce correct recitation.", w: ["leaving it", "exercise, training", "his jaw (the organs of speech)"] },
  34: { r: ["Pronounce the lowered letters thin (tarqiq),", "and beware of thickening the sound of alif,"], c: "The lowered letters are pronounced thin by default; the exceptions are ra and the lam of the name “Allah” in certain positions. Alif is neither heavy nor light by itself: it follows the letter before it — heavy after a heavy letter (“قَالَ”), light after a light one (“كَانَ”). Thickening it after a light letter is a mistake.", w: ["make thin (emphatic imperative)", "a lowered letter", "beware", "thickening"] },
  35: { r: ["and the hamzah in “al-hamdu”, “a‘udhu”, “ihdina”,", "“Allahu”; then the lam in “lillahi”, “lana”,"], c: "Hamzah is a lowered letter and stays light even next to a heavy one: “أَعُوذُ” (before ‘ayn), “اللَّهُ” (before a heavy lam). The lam in “لِلَّهِ” is light: after kasrah the lam of the name “Allah” is thin, and it must not be thickened by analogy with “اللَّهُ”. The lam in “لَنَا” must also be light.", w: ["the lam in “lillahi”", "guide us, 1:6"] },
  36: { r: ["[the lam] in “walyatalattaf”, “wa ‘ala Allahi”, “wa la ad-[dallin]”;", "and the mim in “makhmasah” and in “marad”;"], c: "Lam stays light even beside heavy letters: “وَلْيَتَلَطَّفْ” (before ط), “وَعَلَى اللَّهِ” (before the heavy lam of “Allah”), “وَلَا الضَّالِّينَ” (before ض). Mim is light in “مَخْمَصَةٍ” (next to خ and ص) and in “مَرَضٌ” (next to ر and ض).", w: ["short for “ad-dallin”", "hunger", "illness"] },
  37: { r: ["and the ba in “barq”, “batil”, “bihim”, “bidhi”.", "And be keen to keep the strength (shiddah) and voicing (jahr)"], c: "Ba is light even beside heavy letters (“بَرْقٌ”, “بَاطِلٌ”) and in similar words like “بِهِمْ”, “بِذِي”. Besides lightness, ba must keep its shiddah and jahr so that it does not become a soft “v” or a voiceless “p”.", w: ["lightning", "falsehood", "be keen, watch", "strength", "voicing"] },
  38: { r: ["found in it and in jim, as in “hubb”, “as-sabr”,", "“rabwah”, “ujtuththat”, “hajj”, “al-fajr”."], c: "Examples where ba and jim are easily softened: with shaddah (“حُبِّ”, “حَجِّ”) and with sukun (“الصَّبْرِ”, “رَبْوَةٍ”, “اجْتُثَّتْ”, “الْفَجْرِ”). Jim must not be pronounced like a soft “zh” or “sh”: it is a strong voiced sound.", w: ["love", "patience", "a hill", "uprooted", "hajj", "dawn"] },
  39: { r: ["Make the qalqalah of a [qalqalah letter] clear when it has sukun,", "and at a stop it is clearer still."], c: "ق ط ب ج د with sukun are pronounced with qalqalah. At a stop (temporary sukun) it is clearer, and clearer still when stopping on a doubled letter (“الْحَقّْ”). This gives three degrees: minor (within a word), major (at a stop) and greatest (stopping on a shaddah).", w: ["make clear", "a qalqalah letter", "clearer"] },
  40: { r: ["And [make clear] the ha in “hashasa”, “ahattu”, “al-haqq”,", "and the sin in “mustaqim”, “yastuna”, “yasquna”."], c: "Ha (ح) next to the elevated letters ص, ط, ق is easily distorted into kha (خ) or thickened; it must be pronounced clean and light. Sin next to ت, ط, ق can turn into sad (ص); it must stay light and whistling.", w: ["became manifest (the truth), 12:51", "I encompassed, 27:22", "assault, 22:72", "water (their flocks), 28:23"] },
  41: { r: ["Make ra thin when it carries kasrah,", "and likewise after a kasrah when it has sukun,"], c: "The main cases of light ra: 1) ra with kasrah — “رِزْقًا”, “الْغَارِمِينَ”; 2) ra with sukun after kasrah — “فِرْعَوْنَ”, “مِرْيَةٍ”. In all other cases (fathah, dammah, sukun after fathah or dammah) ra is heavy.", w: ["make thin", "had kasrah", "had sukun"] },
  42: { r: ["as long as it does not come before a letter of elevation", "and the kasrah is original."], c: "Two exceptions for ra with sukun after kasrah. 1) An elevated letter follows ra in the same word: “قِرْطَاسٍ”, “فِرْقَةٍ”, “مِرْصَادًا” — ra is heavy. 2) The kasrah is not original: the kasrah of the connecting hamzah (“ارْجِعُوا”, “ارْكَبْ”) or one added between words (“إِنِ ارْتَبْتُمْ”) — ra is heavy.", w: ["a letter of elevation", "original"] },
  43: { r: ["There is a difference over “firq” because of the kasrah [on qaf];", "and hide the repetition (takrir) when [ra] is doubled."], c: "In “فِرْقٍ” (26:63) the elevated letter ق follows ra, but it carries kasrah, which weakens its elevation, so both tafkhim and tarqiq are allowed. The trill of ra must be hidden, especially with shaddah: the tongue presses once, without vibrating.", w: ["difference of opinion", "part (of the parted sea), 26:63", "hide", "doubled (with shaddah)"] },
  44: { r: ["Thicken the lam of the name “Allah”", "after fathah or dammah, as in “‘abdu-llah”."], c: "The lam of the name “Allah” is heavy after fathah (“قَالَ اللَّهُ”, “شَهِدَ اللَّهُ”) or dammah (“عَبْدُ اللَّهِ”, “رُسُلُ اللَّهِ”), and at the start of reading (“اَللَّهُ”). After kasrah it is light (“بِسْمِ اللَّهِ”, “لِلَّهِ”) — see verse 35.", w: ["thicken", "the name “Allah”"] },
  45: { r: ["Thicken the letters of elevation, and single out", "the covered ones as stronger, as in “qala” and “al-‘asa”."], c: "All seven elevated letters (خص ضغط قظ) are always heavy. The covered letters (ص ض ط ظ) are heavier than the rest: the ص in “الْعَصَا” is heavier than the ق in “قَالَ”. The degree of tafkhim also depends on the vowel: strongest with fathah before alif, weakest with kasrah.", w: ["elevation", "single out", "the letters of itbaq", "stronger"] },
  46: { r: ["Make the covering (itbaq) clear in “ahattu” together with", "“basatta”; and there is a difference over “nakhluqkum”."], c: "In incomplete merging of ط into ت (“أَحَطتُّ”, “بَسَطتَّ”, “فَرَّطتُّمْ”) the covering of ط is kept: the tongue presses against the palate, but ت is what sounds. In “أَلَمْ نَخْلُقكُّمْ” (77:20) there is full merging of ق into ك (preferred) or incomplete merging that keeps the elevation of ق.", w: ["make clear", "I encompassed, 27:22", "you stretched, 5:28", "did We not create you, 77:20"] },
  47: { r: ["Be keen to keep the sukun [pure] in “ja‘alna”,", "“an‘amta”, “al-maghdub” and “dalalna”."], c: "A letter with sukun must stay without a vowel and without an extra “bounce”: the lam in “جَعَلْنَا” and “ضَلَلْنَا”, the mim in “أَنْعَمْتَ”, the ghayn in “الْمَغْضُوبِ”. The lam must not be merged into the nun, and letters without qalqalah must not be given one.", w: ["sukun", "we are lost, 32:10"] },
  48: { r: ["Keep the openness pure in “mahdhuran” and “‘asa”,", "for fear of confusing them with “mahzuran” and “‘asa (ص)”."], c: "ذ and س are open, lowered letters. If they are thickened, “مَحْذُورًا” (“to be feared”, 17:57) turns into “مَحْظُورًا” (“forbidden”, 17:20), and “عَسَى” (“perhaps”) into “عَصَى” (“disobeyed”). An example of how a mistake in an attribute changes the meaning.", w: ["purify", "its confusion"] },
  49: { r: ["Observe the strength (shiddah) of kaf and ta,", "as in “bishirkikum”, “tatawaffahum”, “fitnatan”."], c: "When ك or ت repeat side by side they are easily blurred or one is swallowed. Each must be pronounced distinctly, keeping both shiddah and hams — the light release of air after the sound.", w: ["observe", "your association (shirk), 35:14", "take (the souls)", "trial"] },
  50: { r: ["The first of two identical or same-kind [letters], when it has sukun,", "merge it (idgham), as in “qul rabbi”, “bal la”; but make clear"], c: "Idgham of identical letters (mithlayn): the first carries sukun (“بَل لَّا”, “اضْرِب بِّعَصَاكَ”) and they must merge. Idgham of same-kind letters (mutajanisayn): one makhraj, different attributes (“قَد تَّبَيَّنَ”, “ارْكَب مَّعَنَا”). “قُل رَّبِّ” is an example of close letters (mutaqaribayn), which are also merged. The exceptions follow in the next verse.", w: ["identical letters", "same-kind letters (shared makhraj)", "merge", "make clear (izhar)"] },
  51: { r: ["in “fi yawmin”, “qalu wa hum”, “qul na‘am”,", "“fa-sabbihhu”, “la tuzigh qulubana”, “fa-ltaqamahu”."], c: "Exceptions to idgham: in “فِي يَوْمٍ” and “قَالُوا وَهُمْ” the first letter is a madd letter, so there is no idgham, or the lengthening would disappear. In “قُلْ نَعَمْ” (lam before nun), “فَسَبِّحْهُ” (ha before ha), “لَا تُزِغْ قُلُوبَنَا” (ghayn before qaf) and “فَالْتَقَمَهُ” (lam before ta) the letters are pronounced clearly (izhar).", w: ["and it swallowed him, 37:142", "do not let deviate, 3:8"] },
  52: { r: ["Distinguish dad by elongation and by its makhraj", "from za (ظ); all [words with za] occur"], c: "ض and ظ sound alike (both voiced, soft, elevated and covered). They differ in makhraj (ض: the edge of the tongue with the molars; ظ: the tip of the tongue with the edges of the upper incisors) and in the elongation of ض. Next the author lists the roots written with ظ in the Qur’an; everything else is written with ض.", w: ["elongation (attribute of ض)", "distinguish", "occur"] },
  53: { r: ["in [the roots] “za‘n” (travel), “zill” (shade), “zuhr” (noon), “‘uzm” (greatness), “hifz” (keeping),", "“ayqiz” (awake), “anzir” (respite), “‘azm” (bone), “zahr” (back), “lafz” (uttering)."], c: "The list of roots with ظ begins (about thirty in the Qur’an): “يَوْمَ ظَعْنِكُمْ” (16:80), “ظِلّ” and its derivatives, “وَحِينَ تُظْهِرُونَ”, “عَظِيم” and derivatives, “حِفْظ”, “أَيْقَاظًا” (18:18), “أَنْظِرْنِي”, “عَظْم”, “ظَهْر”, “مَا يَلْفِظُ” (50:18).", w: ["travel, 16:80", "noon", "greatness", "awake, 18:18", "respite", "uttering, 50:18"] },
  54: { r: ["“zahir” (apparent), “laza” (blaze), “shuwaz” (flame), “kazm” (restraint), “zalama” (wronged),", "“ughluz” (be stern), “zalam” (darkness), “zufr” (claw), “intazir” (wait), “zama’” (thirst)."], c: "The list continues: “ظَاهِر” and its derivatives (“ظَاهَرُوا”, “تَظَاهَرَا”), “لَظَى” (70:15), “شُوَاظٌ” (55:35), “وَالْكَاظِمِينَ”, “ظُلْم” — one of the most frequent roots, “وَاغْلُظْ”, “ظُلُمَات”, “ذِي ظُفُرٍ” (6:146), “فَانْتَظِرُوا”, “ظَمَأٌ”.", w: ["blaze (a name of Hell), 70:15", "flames, 55:35", "restraining (anger)", "claw, 6:146", "thirst"] },
  55: { r: ["“azfara” (gave victory), “zann” (assumption) in any form, “wa‘z” (admonition) — except", "“‘idin”; and “zalla” in an-Nahl and az-Zukhruf alike,"], c: "“أَظْفَرَكُمْ” (48:24); “ظَنّ” in every form; “وَعْظ” and its derivatives — except “عِضِينَ” (15:91), written with ض (from “to cut into parts”). “ظَلَّ” meaning “became, remained” — in an-Nahl (16:58) and az-Zukhruf (43:17): “ظَلَّ وَجْهُهُ مُسْوَدًّا”.", w: ["gave victory, 48:24", "assumption, opinion", "in any form", "in parts, 15:91", "alike"] },
  56: { r: ["“zalta”, “zaltum”, and in ar-Rum “zallu”,", "as in al-Hijr; “zallat”, and in ash-Shu‘ara’ “nazallu”."], c: "The remaining forms of the verb “ظَلَّ”: “ظَلْتَ عَلَيْهِ عَاكِفًا” (20:97), “فَظَلْتُمْ تَفَكَّهُونَ” (56:65), “لَظَلُّوا” (30:51), “فَظَلُّوا فِيهِ يَعْرُجُونَ” (15:14), “فَظَلَّتْ أَعْنَاقُهُمْ” (26:4), “فَنَظَلُّ لَهَا عَاكِفِينَ” (26:71).", w: ["you remained, 20:97", "we remain, 26:71"] },
  57: { r: ["“yazlalna”, “mahzuran” together with “al-muhtazir”,", "“kunta fazzan”, and every [form of] “nazar” (looking),"], c: "“فَيَظْلَلْنَ رَوَاكِدَ” (42:33), “مَحْظُورًا” — “forbidden, restricted” (17:20), “كَهَشِيمِ الْمُحْتَظِرِ” (54:31), “وَلَوْ كُنْتَ فَظًّا” — “harsh” (3:159). The root “نَظَر” (to look, to wait) in every form, except the exceptions of the next verse.", w: ["remain [still], 42:33", "the builder of an enclosure, 54:31", "harsh, 3:159", "looking"] },
  58: { r: ["except in [the suras] “Waylun” and “Hal [ata]” and the first “nadirah”,", "and “ghayz” (rage) — but not in ar-Ra‘d and Hud: [the exception is] limited."], c: "Exceptions to the root “نَظَر”, written with ض (meaning “radiance”): “نَضْرَةَ النَّعِيمِ” (83:24, the sura that begins with “وَيْلٌ”), “نَضْرَةً وَسُرُورًا” (76:11, the sura that begins with “هَلْ أَتَى”), and the first word of “وُجُوهٌ يَوْمَئِذٍ نَاضِرَةٌ” (75:22); the second, “إِلَى رَبِّهَا نَاظِرَةٌ”, is with ظ. “غَيْظ” (rage) is with ظ, but “وَمَا تَغِيضُ الْأَرْحَامُ” (13:8) and “وَغِيضَ الْمَاءُ” (11:44) are with ض (“to decrease”).", w: ["radiant, 75:22", "rage", "restricted (exception)"] },
  59: { r: ["“hazz” (portion) — [with za], not “hadd” (urging) to feed,", "and over “zanin” there is a lofty, well-known difference."], c: "“حَظّ” — “portion, share” — is with ظ; “وَلَا يَحُضُّ عَلَى طَعَامِ الْمِسْكِينِ” — “does not urge” — is with ض. In 81:24 there are two authentic readings: “بِظَنِينٍ” (“suspected”) by Ibn Kathir, Abu ‘Amr, al-Kisa’i and Ruways, and “بِضَنِينٍ” (“withholding”) by the rest, including Hafs.", w: ["portion, share", "urging", "suspected, 81:24", "lofty, i.e. well-known"] },
  60: { r: ["If they meet, making them clear is binding:", "“anqada zahrak”, “ya‘addu az-zalim”."], c: "When ض and ظ stand side by side, each is pronounced clearly from its own makhraj, without merging: “الَّذِي أَنْقَضَ ظَهْرَكَ” (94:3), “وَيَوْمَ يَعَضُّ الظَّالِمُ” (25:27).", w: ["met", "clarity", "weighed down, 94:3", "bites, 25:27"] },
  61: { r: ["[make clear] “udturra” together with “wa‘azta” and “afadtum”,", "and keep the ha pure in “jibahuhum”, “‘alayhim”."], c: "ض before ط (“اضْطُرَّ”), ظ before ت (“أَوَعَظْتَ”, 26:136) and ض before ت (“أَفَضْتُمْ”, 24:14) are pronounced clearly, without idgham. Ha (ه) is a weak letter (whispered and soft); when it repeats or is surrounded by similar sounds (“جِبَاهُهُمْ”, 9:35; “عَلَيْهِمْ”) it is easily lost, so it must be pronounced clean and clear.", w: ["was compelled", "you admonished, 26:136", "you spread, 24:14", "purify", "their foreheads, 9:35"] },
  62: { r: ["Show the ghunnah of nun and of", "mim when they are doubled; and hide (ikhfa’)"], c: "Nun and mim with shaddah (“إِنَّ”, “ثُمَّ”, “النَّاسِ”, “الْجَنَّةِ”) are pronounced with full ghunnah lasting about two counts — its strongest degree.", w: ["show", "ghunnah", "were doubled", "hide"] },
  63: { r: ["the mim with sukun, with ghunnah, before", "ba, by the view chosen by the masters of recitation."], c: "Labial ikhfa’: mim with sukun before ba (“تَرْمِيهِمْ بِحِجَارَةٍ”, “وَهُمْ بِالْآخِرَةِ”) is hidden with ghunnah. This is the view of most reciters, though some read it with izhar. Mim with sukun before mim is merged with ghunnah (“لَهُمْ مَا”).", w: ["has sukun", "before, at", "the preferred view", "the experts of recitation"] },
  64: { r: ["Before the remaining letters show it clearly (izhar),", "and beware lest it hide before waw and fa."], c: "Labial izhar: mim with sukun before every letter except ب and م is pronounced clearly: “أَمْ لَمْ”, “لَكُمْ دِينُكُمْ”. Take special care before و and ف (“عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا”, “هُمْ فِيهَا”): their makharij are close to the lips, so the mim is easily hidden.", w: ["show it (the mim)", "the remaining", "it hides"] },
  65: { r: ["The rule of tanwin and [sakin] nun is", "izhar, idgham, iqlab and ikhfa’."], c: "Nun with sukun and tanwin have four rules, depending on the letter that follows: izhar (clear pronunciation), idgham (merging), iqlab (conversion into mim) and ikhfa’ (concealment).", w: ["tanwin (nunation)", "is found, occurs", "clear pronunciation", "merging", "conversion", "concealment"] },
  66: { r: ["Before a throat letter make it clear; and merge", "into lam and ra without ghunnah — necessarily."], c: "Throat izhar: before the six throat letters (ء ه ع ح غ خ) nun and tanwin are pronounced clearly, without ghunnah: “مَنْ آمَنَ”, “عَذَابٌ أَلِيمٌ”, “يَنْهَوْنَ”. Merging without ghunnah (complete) happens before ل and ر: “مِنْ لَدُنْهُ”, “غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ”.", w: ["a throat letter", "merge", "necessarily"] },
  67: { r: ["Merge with ghunnah into the letters of “yumin”,", "except within one word, as in “dunya” — so they designated."], c: "Merging with ghunnah happens before the four letters ي و م ن (يُومِنُ): “مَنْ يَقُولُ”, “مِنْ وَالٍ”, “مِنْ مَاءٍ”, “مِنْ نُورٍ”. Idgham only happens between two words. If nun and one of these letters are in the same word, it is absolute izhar: “الدُّنْيَا”, “بُنْيَانٌ”, “قِنْوَانٌ”, “صِنْوَانٌ”.", w: ["merge", "mnemonic of the letters ي و م ن", "within one word", "they designated, named"] },
  68: { r: ["Conversion (iqlab) is before ba, with ghunnah; likewise", "ikhfa’ before the remaining letters is taken [with ghunnah]."], c: "Iqlab: before ب, nun with sukun and tanwin turn into a mim hidden with ghunnah: “مِنْ بَعْدِ”, “أَنْبِئْهُمْ”, “سَمِيعٌ بَصِيرٌ”. Ikhfa’: before the remaining 15 letters (ت ث ج د ذ ز س ش ص ض ط ظ ف ق ك) the nun is concealed — a sound between izhar and idgham, with ghunnah: “مِنْ قَبْلِ”, “أَنْتُمْ”, “رِيحًا صَرْصَرًا”.", w: ["conversion", "concealment", "is taken, transmitted"] },
  69: { r: ["Madd comes as necessary (lazim), obligatory (wajib)", "and permissible (ja’iz) — in it both lengthening and shortening are established."], c: "Besides natural madd (2 counts), lengthening caused by a hamzah or a sukun is of three kinds: lazim, wajib and ja’iz. In permissible madd both lengthening and shortening (qasr) are transmitted.", w: ["lengthening", "necessary, fixed", "obligatory", "permissible", "shortening"] },
  70: { r: ["It is necessary if after a madd letter comes", "a sukun in both states; and it is drawn long."], c: "Necessary madd: a permanent sukun follows the madd letter, both when continuing and when stopping: “الضَّالِّينَ”, “الْحَاقَّةُ”, “آلْآنَ”, “الٓمٓ”. All reciters lengthen it to 6 counts.", w: ["both states: continuing and stopping", "length (6 counts)"] },
  71: { r: ["Obligatory, if [the madd letter] comes before a hamzah,", "connected, when they are joined in one word."], c: "Obligatory connected madd: the madd letter and the hamzah are in one word — “جَاءَ”, “السَّمَاءِ”, “سُوءَ”, “جِيءَ”. In the narration of Hafs via ash-Shatibiyyah it is 4 or 5 counts, and up to 6 when stopping on the hamzah.", w: ["hamzah", "connected, in one word", "joined together"] },
  72: { r: ["And permissible when it comes separated,", "or when the sukun arises at a stop — in all cases."], c: "Permissible madd: 1) separated — the madd letter ends one word and the hamzah begins the next: “بِمَا أُنْزِلَ”, “قُوا أَنْفُسَكُمْ”; for Hafs via ash-Shatibiyyah 4–5 counts, via at-Tayyibah 2 is also allowed. 2) Madd before a sukun caused by stopping: “الْعَالَمِينَ”, “نَسْتَعِينُ” — 2, 4 or 6 counts. “Musjalan” means “without restriction,” so madd lin is included (“خَوْفٌ”, “الْبَيْتِ”).", w: ["separated, across two words", "arose temporarily", "at a stop", "without restriction"] },
  73: { r: ["After you have mastered the tajwid of the letters,", "you must know the stops"], c: "Knowing where to stop is the next step after correct pronunciation: a wrong stop can distort the meaning of a verse. ‘Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) explained tartil as “the tajwid of the letters and knowing the places of stopping.”", w: ["your tajwid", "necessarily", "stops"] },
  74: { r: ["and starts. They are divided, then,", "into three: complete (tamm), sufficient (kafi) and good (hasan)."], c: "Ibtida’ — starting again after a stop — also needs knowledge: one must start where the meaning is not distorted. Stops by choice are of three kinds: complete, sufficient and good.", w: ["starting (after a stop)", "are divided", "complete", "sufficient", "good"] },
  75: { r: ["They are for where [the meaning] is complete; and if there is no", "link [to what follows], or only in meaning, then start [from the next]."], c: "All three are stops on a complete meaning. If there is no link at all with what follows — neither grammatical nor in meaning — the stop is complete; if the link is only in meaning, it is sufficient. In both cases one may stop and start from the next word.", w: ["was completed", "connection", "in meaning", "start"] },
  76: { r: ["That is complete, that is sufficient; but where the link is in wording, forbid [starting from the next],", "except at the ends of verses — allow it; that is good."], c: "If the stopping place is also grammatically linked to what follows, the stop is good: one may stop, but not start from the next word — one must go back. The exception is the end of a verse, where one may both stop and start from the next, following the Sunnah: the Prophet ﷺ paused at every verse.", w: ["forbid", "ends of verses", "allow"] },
  77: { r: ["A stop where it is incomplete is ugly (qabih); at it", "one stops only when forced, and starts again from before it."], c: "An ugly stop is where the meaning is incomplete (“بِسْمِ”, “الْحَمْدُ”), or worse, where a distorted meaning results (“إِنَّ اللَّهَ لَا يَسْتَحْيِي”). It is allowed only under compulsion — running out of breath, a cough — after which one goes back and starts from a suitable place.", w: ["ugly, blameworthy", "under compulsion", "one starts", "from what precedes it"] },
  78: { r: ["In the Qur’an there is no obligatory stop", "and none forbidden, except one that has a reason."], c: "No stop is obligatory in itself (so that omitting it is a sin), and none is forbidden. The marks “lazim” and “la” in mushafs are recommendations of the experts of waqf. Sin arises only from a cause — for example, stopping deliberately to distort the meaning.", w: ["obligatory", "forbidden", "cause"] },
  79: { r: ["Know what is written separately, joined, and the [open] ta", "in the mushaf of the Imam, in what occurs in it."], c: "“The mushaf of the Imam” is the mushaf copied by order of ‘Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him); its spelling is the model for all later mushafs. Knowing separate and joined writing matters for stopping: when forced or tested, one may stop on the first word only if it is written separately.", w: ["the mushaf of ‘Uthman", "occurred, came"] },
  80: { r: ["Write “an la” separately in ten words:", "with “malja’” and “la ilaha illa”,"], c: "“أَنْ” + “لَا” are usually written joined (“أَلَّا”), but separately in ten places: “أَنْ لَا مَلْجَأَ مِنَ اللَّهِ” (9:118), “وَأَنْ لَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا هُوَ” (11:14), and the places named in verses 81–82. The place “أَنْ لَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا أَنْتَ” (21:87) is transmitted with a difference.", w: ["write separately", "refuge, 9:118"] },
  81: { r: ["“ta‘budu” in Ya-Sin and the second in Hud; “la", "yushrikna”, “tushrik”, “yadkhulanna”, “ta‘lu ‘ala”,"], c: "More places of separate “أَنْ لَا”: “أَنْ لَا تَعْبُدُوا الشَّيْطَانَ” (36:60), “أَنْ لَا تَعْبُدُوا إِلَّا اللَّهَ إِنِّي أَخَافُ” (11:26 — the second in Hud; the first, 11:2, is joined), “أَنْ لَا يُشْرِكْنَ” (60:12), “أَنْ لَا تُشْرِكْ بِي” (22:26), “أَنْ لَا يَدْخُلَنَّهَا” (68:24), “وَأَنْ لَا تَعْلُوا عَلَى اللَّهِ” (44:19).", w: ["the second [place] in Surat Hud, 11:26"] },
  82: { r: ["“an la yaqulu”, “la aqula”. “In ma”", "in ar-Ra‘d [separately]; with fathah — join it. And “‘an ma”"], c: "The last places of “أَنْ لَا”: “أَنْ لَا يَقُولُوا عَلَى اللَّهِ إِلَّا الْحَقَّ” (7:169) and “حَقِيقٌ عَلَى أَنْ لَا أَقُولَ” (7:105). “إِنْ مَا” is separate only in “وَإِنْ مَا نُرِيَنَّكَ” (13:40); elsewhere it is “وَإِمَّا”. “أَمَّا” with fathah (“أَمْ” + “مَا”) is joined everywhere: “أَمَّا اشْتَمَلَتْ” (6:143), “أَمَّاذَا كُنْتُمْ” (27:84).", w: ["write joined", "with fathah (on the hamzah)"] },
  83: { r: ["“nuhu” — write separately; “min ma” in ar-Rum and an-Nisa’,", "with a difference in al-Munafiqun; “am man” in “assasa”,"], c: "“عَنْ مَا نُهُوا عَنْهُ” (7:166) is separate; elsewhere it is joined: “عَمَّا”. “مِنْ مَا” is separate in “مِنْ مَا مَلَكَتْ أَيْمَانُكُمْ” (30:28 and 4:25); in al-Munafiqun, “مِنْ مَا رَزَقْنَاكُمْ” (63:10) has a difference; elsewhere it is “مِمَّا”. “أَمْ مَنْ” is separate in “أَمْ مَنْ أَسَّسَ بُنْيَانَهُ” (9:109) and the places of the next verse.", w: ["they were forbidden, 7:166", "founded, 9:109"] },
  84: { r: ["in Fussilat, an-Nisa’ and [the sura of] “dhibh”. “Haythu ma”", "and “an lam” with fathah [separately]; “inna ma” with kasrah —"], c: "“أَمْ مَنْ” is also separate in three places: “أَمْ مَنْ يَأْتِي آمِنًا” (41:40), “أَمْ مَنْ يَكُونُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَكِيلًا” (4:109), “أَمْ مَنْ خَلَقْنَا” (37:11 — Surat as-Saffat, which mentions “بِذِبْحٍ عَظِيمٍ”); elsewhere it is “أَمَّنْ”. “حَيْثُ مَا” is always separate (2:144, 2:150). “أَنْ لَمْ” with fathah is always separate: “ذَلِكَ أَنْ لَمْ يَكُنْ رَبُّكَ”. “إِنَّ مَا” with kasrah — in the next verse.", w: ["wherever, 2:144", "sacrifice — pointing to Surat as-Saffat"] },
  85: { r: ["in al-An‘am; and with fathah, before “yad‘una” in both places;", "and there is a difference in al-Anfal and an-Nahl."], c: "“إِنَّ مَا تُوعَدُونَ لَآتٍ” (6:134) is separate; elsewhere “إِنَّمَا” is joined. “أَنَّ مَا” with fathah is separate before “يَدْعُونَ”: “وَأَنَّ مَا يَدْعُونَ مِنْ دُونِهِ هُوَ الْبَاطِلُ” (22:62 and 31:30). With a difference: “وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّمَا غَنِمْتُمْ” (8:41) and “إِنَّمَا عِنْدَ اللَّهِ هُوَ خَيْرٌ” (16:95).", w: ["“yad‘una” in both places: 22:62 and 31:30"] },
  86: { r: ["and “kulli ma sa’altumuhu” [separately]; there is a difference", "in “ruddu”, and likewise “qul bi’sama”; describe as joined"], c: "“كُلِّ مَا” is separate in “وَآتَاكُمْ مِنْ كُلِّ مَا سَأَلْتُمُوهُ” (14:34); with a difference in “كُلَّ مَا رُدُّوا إِلَى الْفِتْنَةِ” (4:91); elsewhere it is “كُلَّمَا”. “بِئْسَمَا” in “قُلْ بِئْسَمَا يَأْمُرُكُمْ” (2:93) has a difference; it is joined in the two places of the next verse, and separate elsewhere: “لَبِئْسَ مَا”.", w: ["you asked Him, 14:34", "they were returned, 4:91", "describe, i.e. indicate"] },
  87: { r: ["“khalaftumuni” and “ishtaraw”. Write “fi ma” separately [in]:", "“uhiya”, “afadtum”, “ishtahat”, “yablu” — both,"], c: "“بِئْسَمَا خَلَفْتُمُونِي” (7:150) and “بِئْسَمَا اشْتَرَوْا” (2:90) are joined. “فِي مَا” is separate in eleven places: “فِي مَا أُوحِيَ” (6:145), “فِي مَا أَفَضْتُمْ” (24:14), “فِي مَا اشْتَهَتْ” (21:102), “لِيَبْلُوَكُمْ فِي مَا” (5:48 and 6:165), and the places of the next verse.", w: ["you replaced me, 7:150", "desired, 21:102"] },
  88: { r: ["the second “fa‘alna”, al-Waqi‘ah, ar-Rum, both [places]", "in Tanzil, ash-Shu‘ara’; and join the rest."], c: "“فِي مَا” is also separate in: “فِي مَا فَعَلْنَ فِي أَنْفُسِهِنَّ مِنْ مَعْرُوفٍ” (2:240 — the second in al-Baqarah), “فِي مَا لَا تَعْلَمُونَ” (56:61), “فِي مَا رَزَقْنَاكُمْ” (30:28), “فِي مَا هُمْ فِيهِ” and “فِي مَا كَانُوا فِيهِ” (39:3 and 39:46 — Surat Tanzil, i.e. az-Zumar), “فِي مَا هَاهُنَا” (26:146). Everywhere else “فِيمَا” is joined.", w: ["both places in Surat Tanzil (az-Zumar)", "write joined"] },
  89: { r: ["“fa-aynama” [in al-Baqarah], as in an-Nahl — join it; and with a difference", "it is described in az-Zullah, al-Ahzab and an-Nisa’."], c: "“أَيْنَمَا” is joined in “فَأَيْنَمَا تُوَلُّوا فَثَمَّ وَجْهُ اللَّهِ” (2:115) and “أَيْنَمَا يُوَجِّهْهُ” (16:76). With a difference: “أَيْنَ مَا كُنْتُمْ تَعْبُدُونَ” (26:92 — Surat ash-Shu‘ara’, which mentions “the day of the canopy”), “أَيْنَمَا ثُقِفُوا” (33:61), “أَيْنَمَا تَكُونُوا يُدْرِكْكُمُ الْمَوْتُ” (4:78). Elsewhere it is separate: “أَيْنَ مَا”.", w: ["“the canopy” — pointing to Surat ash-Shu‘ara’ (26:189)", "described"] },
  90: { r: ["Join “fa-illam” in Hud, “allan” in “naj‘ala”", "and “najma‘a”; “kayla” in “tahzanu”, “ta’saw ‘ala”,"], c: "“فَإِلَّمْ يَسْتَجِيبُوا لَكُمْ” (11:14) is joined; elsewhere “فَإِنْ لَمْ” is separate. “أَلَّنْ” is joined in two places: “أَلَّنْ نَجْعَلَ لَكُمْ مَوْعِدًا” (18:48) and “أَلَّنْ نَجْمَعَ عِظَامَهُ” (75:3); elsewhere “أَنْ لَنْ”. “لِكَيْلَا” is joined in “لِكَيْلَا تَحْزَنُوا” (3:153), “لِكَيْلَا تَأْسَوْا” (57:23), “لِكَيْلَا يَكُونَ عَلَيْكَ حَرَجٌ” (33:50) and in Surat al-Hajj (22:5).", w: ["“and if not” (joined), 11:14", "“so that not” (joined)"] },
  91: { r: ["[in] al-Hajj and “‘alayka harajun”. Written separately by them:", "“‘an man yasha’u”, “‘an man tawalla”, “yawma hum”."], c: "“لِكَيْلَا يَعْلَمَ” (22:5) and “لِكَيْلَا يَكُونَ عَلَيْكَ حَرَجٌ” (33:50) are joined; elsewhere “لِكَيْ لَا”. “عَنْ مَنْ” is separate in “وَيَصْرِفُهُ عَنْ مَنْ يَشَاءُ” (24:43) and “فَأَعْرِضْ عَنْ مَنْ تَوَلَّى” (53:29). “يَوْمَ هُمْ” is separate in “يَوْمَ هُمْ بَارِزُونَ” (40:16) and “يَوْمَ هُمْ عَلَى النَّارِ يُفْتَنُونَ” (51:13).", w: ["their separate writing", "turned away, 53:29"] },
  92: { r: ["And the “li” of “mali” before “hadha”, “alladhina”, “ha’ula’” [is separate];", "“tahina” in the Imam — join it, [it is said], but this was judged a mistake."], c: "The preposition lam of “مَالِ” is written apart from the following word in four places: “مَالِ هَذَا الْكِتَابِ” (18:49), “مَالِ هَذَا الرَّسُولِ” (25:7), “فَمَالِ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا” (70:36), “فَمَالِ هَؤُلَاءِ الْقَوْمِ” (4:78). Some claimed that in the mushaf of the Imam the ta of “وَلَاتَ حِينَ” (38:3) is joined to “حِينَ” (“تَحِينَ”); this view was judged mistaken, and the accepted writing is separate: “وَلَاتَ حِينَ”.", w: ["deemed a mistake, weak", "the mushaf of the Imam"] },
  93: { r: ["Join “wazanuhum” and “kaluhum”;", "likewise do not separate “al”, “ya” and “ha”."], c: "“وَإِذَا كَالُوهُمْ أَوْ وَزَنُوهُمْ” (83:3): the pronoun “هُمْ” is written joined to the verb (with no alif after the waw), so one stops only on the whole word. The article “ال” (“الْأَرْض”), the vocative particle “يَا” (“يَأَيُّهَا”, “يَآدَمُ”) and the particle “هَا” (“هَأَنْتُمْ”, “هَؤُلَاءِ”) are also written joined.", w: ["they weigh for them, 83:3", "they measure for them, 83:3", "do not separate"] },
  94: { r: ["“Rahmat” in az-Zukhruf he wrote with ta,", "[and in] al-A‘raf, ar-Rum, Hud, Kaf and al-Baqarah."], c: "“رَحْمَت” with an open ta occurs in seven places: 2:218, 7:56, 11:73, 19:2, 30:50 and twice in 43:32. In the narration of Hafs one stops on these words with ta (“رَحْمَتْ”), not ha. “Kaf” is Surat Kaf-Ha-Ya-‘Ayn-Sad, i.e. Maryam.", w: ["wrote it", "Surat Maryam (begins with “كهيعص”)"] },
  95: { r: ["“Ni‘mat” in it (al-Baqarah); three in an-Nahl; in Ibrahim", "the last two; in al-‘Uqud the second, [with] “hamma”;"], c: "“نِعْمَت” with an open ta occurs in eleven places: al-Baqarah (2:231); an-Nahl — the last three (16:72, 16:83, 16:114); Ibrahim — the last two (14:28, 14:34); al-Ma’idah — the second, “نِعْمَتَ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ إِذْ هَمَّ قَوْمٌ” (5:11); and the four places of the next verse.", w: ["Surat Ibrahim", "Surat al-Ma’idah (begins with “أَوْفُوا بِالْعُقُودِ”)", "“when [a people] intended”, 5:11"] },
  96: { r: ["Luqman, then Fatir, as with at-Tur,", "Al ‘Imran; “la‘nat” in it and in an-Nur."], c: "The list of “نِعْمَت” ends with 31:31, 35:3, 52:29 and 3:103. “لَعْنَت” with an open ta occurs in two places: “فَنَجْعَلْ لَعْنَتَ اللَّهِ عَلَى الْكَاذِبِينَ” (3:61) and “أَنَّ لَعْنَتَ اللَّهِ عَلَيْهِ” (24:7).", w: ["curse", "in it, i.e. in Al ‘Imran"] },
  97: { r: ["And “imra’at” in Yusuf, Al ‘Imran, al-Qasas,", "at-Tahrim; “ma‘siyat” belongs [only] to “Qad sami‘a”."], c: "“امْرَأَت” with an open ta is used where a wife is mentioned together with her husband (seven places): “امْرَأَتُ الْعَزِيزِ” (12:30, 12:51), “امْرَأَتُ عِمْرَانَ” (3:35), “امْرَأَتُ فِرْعَوْنَ” (28:9 and 66:11), “امْرَأَتَ نُوحٍ وَامْرَأَتَ لُوطٍ” (66:10). “مَعْصِيَتِ الرَّسُولِ” occurs twice in Surat al-Mujadilah (58:8, 58:9), which begins with “قَدْ سَمِعَ”.", w: ["wife", "disobedience", "is specific to"] },
  98: { r: ["“Shajarat” in ad-Dukhan; “sunnat” in Fatir —", "all [three], and in al-Anfal, and one place in Ghafir."], c: "“إِنَّ شَجَرَتَ الزَّقُّومِ” (44:43). “سُنَّت” with an open ta occurs in five places: three times in 35:43 (“سُنَّتَ الْأَوَّلِينَ”, “لِسُنَّتِ اللَّهِ تَبْدِيلًا”, “لِسُنَّتِ اللَّهِ تَحْوِيلًا”), “فَقَدْ مَضَتْ سُنَّتُ الْأَوَّلِينَ” (8:38) and “سُنَّتَ اللَّهِ الَّتِي قَدْ خَلَتْ فِي عِبَادِهِ” (40:85).", w: ["tree, 44:43", "way, custom", "one place in Surat Ghafir, 40:85"] },
  99: { r: ["“qurratu ‘ayn”, “jannat” in al-Waqi‘ah,", "“fitrat”, “baqiyyat”, “ibnat” and “kalimat” —"], c: "“قُرَّتُ عَيْنٍ لِي وَلَكَ” (28:9), “وَجَنَّتُ نَعِيمٍ” (56:89), “فِطْرَتَ اللَّهِ” (30:30), “بَقِيَّتُ اللَّهِ خَيْرٌ لَكُمْ” (11:86), “وَمَرْيَمَ ابْنَتَ عِمْرَانَ” (66:12), “وَتَمَّتْ كَلِمَتُ رَبِّكَ الْحُسْنَى” (7:137).", w: ["comfort of the eye, 28:9", "natural disposition, 30:30", "what remains, 11:86"] },
  100: { r: ["the middle one of al-A‘raf. And everything over which they differ", "as plural or singular is known [to be written] with ta."], c: "“كَلِمَتُ رَبِّكَ الْحُسْنَى” (7:137) is the middle of three occurrences of this word in al-A‘raf. The general rule: a word that some reciters read as plural and others as singular is written with an open ta, so that the script holds both readings: “كَلِمَتُ رَبِّكَ” (6:115, 10:33, 10:96, 40:6), “غَيَابَتِ الْجُبِّ” (12:10, 12:15), “آيَاتٌ لِلسَّائِلِينَ” (12:7), “فِي الْغُرُفَاتِ” (34:37), “عَلَى بَيِّنَتٍ” (35:40), “مِنْ ثَمَرَاتٍ” (41:47), “جِمَالَتٌ صُفْرٌ” (77:33).", w: ["the middle one", "in plural and singular", "is known"] },
  101: { r: ["Start the connecting hamzah of a verb with dammah", "if the third letter of the verb has [a permanent] dammah."], c: "The connecting hamzah is pronounced only when starting; in connected reading it drops. In a verb its vowel depends on the third letter: if the third letter has a permanent dammah, start with dammah: “اُدْعُوا”, “اُنْظُرْ”, “اُخْرُجْ”, “اُسْتُضْعِفُوا”.", w: ["start", "the connecting hamzah", "the third [letter]"] },
  102: { r: ["Give it kasrah when [the third letter has] kasrah or fathah; and in", "nouns other than [the article] “al”, its vowel is kasrah — as in"], c: "If the third letter of the verb has kasrah (“اِضْرِبْ”, “اِرْجِعِي”) or fathah (“اِذْهَبْ”, “اِعْلَمُوا”), start with kasrah. If the dammah is temporary — the stem has kasrah (“اِمْشُوا”, “اِئْتُوا”, “اِقْضُوا”) — also start with kasrah. In the article “ال” the connecting hamzah takes fathah (“اَلْحَمْدُ”), and in nouns kasrah (listed in the next verse).", w: ["give it kasrah", "nouns", "except the article “al”"] },
  103: { r: ["“ibn” with “ibnah”, “imru’”, “ithnan”,", "“imra’ah”, “ism” and “ithnatan”."], c: "Seven nouns in the Qur’an have a connecting hamzah that is read with kasrah when starting: “اِبْنٌ”, “اِبْنَتٌ”, “اِمْرُؤٌ”, “اِثْنَانِ”, “اِمْرَأَةٌ”, “اِسْمٌ”, “اِثْنَتَانِ”. Verbal nouns of five- and six-letter verbs belong here too: “اِسْتِغْفَارًا”, “اِنْتِقَامٌ”.", w: ["son", "man", "two", "name"] },
  104: { r: ["Beware of stopping with the full vowel,", "unless you do rawm: then [only] part of the vowel,"], c: "The basic rule of stopping is to stop with sukun; stopping with the full vowel is a mistake. The exception is rawm: pronouncing about a third of the vowel in a weakened voice heard only by someone close. Rawm is done with kasrah and dammah.", w: ["beware", "you did rawm", "part"] },
  105: { r: ["except with fathah or nasb. And do ishmam —", "a sign [with the lips] of dammah — in raf‘ and dammah."], c: "Rawm is not done with fathah and nasb: fathah is light, and a part of it cannot be isolated. Ishmam is rounding the lips right after the sukun, silently, as a sign of dammah: it is seen but not heard. It is done only with dammah — case ending (raf‘) or fixed: “نَسْتَعِينُ”, “مِنْ قَبْلُ”.", w: ["do ishmam", "a sign", "raf‘ — the nominative case", "fixed dammah"] },
  106: { r: ["My versified “Introduction” is now complete —", "from me to the reciter of the Qur’an, as a gift."], c: "The author ends the poem and calls it a “taqdimah” — a gift, an offering to the reciter of the Qur’an. There is wordplay between “muqaddimah” (introduction) and “taqdimah” (gift).", w: ["was completed", "my versified work", "a gift, an offering"] },
  107: { r: ["Its verses number “qaf” and “zay”;", "whoever masters tajwid attains right guidance."], c: "By abjad (the numerical value of letters) ق = 100 and ز = 7, i.e. 107 verses. Common editions have 109; scholars explain the difference in various ways — for example, that the author did not count the opening verse or the closing praise. Mastery of tajwid leads to “rushd”: rightness in reciting the Qur’an and in religion.", w: ["its verses", "ق (100) + ز (7) in abjad", "will attain", "right guidance"] },
  108: { r: ["Praise be to Allah as its seal,", "and then blessings and peace"], c: "The poem ends as it began: with praise of Allah. Beginning and ending with praise is a sign of a blessed work.", w: ["conclusion, seal"] },
  109: { r: ["upon the chosen Prophet, his family,", "his companions and those who follow his way."], c: "The closing blessing upon the Prophet ﷺ, his family, his companions and all who follow his example. “Minwal” literally means “a loom,” figuratively “a pattern, a way.”", w: ["the chosen one", "followers", "his pattern, way (literally “loom”)"] },
};
